SEN, ROALD', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Amundsen: The Greatest Expedition (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hagen, Pal Sverre', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-01T11:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannette Catsoulis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannette', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Catsoulis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b2024c2-2fc0-5c4d-a097-a74dd86963fc', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b2024c2-2fc0-5c4d-a097-a74dd86963fc'}, {'abstract': 'In this drama, a widow rises out of grief to protest a threat to her village.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/01/movies/this-is-not-a-burial-its-a-resurrection-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this drama, a widow rises out of grief to protest a threat to her village.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The South African actress Mary Twala Mhlongo (who died last summer at 80) becomes an avatar of grief in “This Is Not a Burial, It’s a Resurrection.” With a gorgeously wizened face that feels weighted down by all that she has seen, Mhlongo plays a widow, Mantoa, whose miner son has just died. At first she sits at night by the radio, listening to obituaries and seemingly waiting for her own maker. But she surges into indignant action when a (true-to-life) dam project threatens her village with erasure, and desecration.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/thisisnot1/merlin_185549568_c991ef5b-c9d9-4a9d-a365-c1de7ebd7d48-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 426, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/thisisnot1/merlin_185549568_c991ef5b-c9d9-4a9d-a365-c1de7ebd7d48-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 426}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/thisisnot1/merlin_185549568_c991ef5b-c9d9-4a9d-a365-c1de7ebd7d48-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 728, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/thisisnot1/merlin_185549568_c991ef5b-c9d9-4a9d-a365-c1de7ebd7d48-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1455, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/thisisnot1/thisisnot1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/thisisnot1/thisisnot1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/thisisnot1/thisisnot1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘This Is Not a Burial, It’s a Resurrection’ Review: Bringing Out the Dead', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Pick', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bringing Out the Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "This Is Not a Burial, It's a Resurrection (Movie)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mosese, Lemohang Jeremia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-01T11:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicolas Rapold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicolas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rapold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f45d2ecf-b11a-5c0a-bd0c-1185271d50da', 'word_count': 279, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f45d2ecf-b11a-5c0a-bd0c-1185271d50da'}, {'abstract': 'In this Oscar-nominated film, a Syrian refugee agrees to become a piece of art in exchange for passage to Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/01/movies/the-man-who-sold-his-skin-review.html', 'snippet': 'In this Oscar-nominated film, a Syrian refugee agrees to become a piece of art in exchange for passage to Europe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Art satire meets immigration drama in the Tunisian director Kaouther Ben Hania’s “The Man Who Sold His Skin.” Ben Hania repurposes a real-life chapter from the annals of the art world, when the Belgian artist Wim Delvoye tattooed the back of a man, and then sold it as art. What sounds like a recipe for trouble — what about the human who’s the canvas? — is exactly where the movie lives, spinning a prickly cautionary tale of exploitation and commodification.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/manwho1/merlin_185549475_dd16eae0-8742-4640-a507-b700c6d87013-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/manwho1/merlin_185549475_dd16eae0-8742-4640-a507-b700c6d87013-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/manwho1/merlin_185549475_dd16eae0-8742-4640-a507-b700c6d87013-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/manwho1/merlin_185549475_dd16eae0-8742-4640-a507-b700c6d87013-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 1920, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/manwho1/manwho1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/manwho1/manwho1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/03/31/arts/manwho1/manwho1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Man Who Sold His Skin’ Review: The Artwork Has Legs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Man Who Sold His Skin', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ben Hania, Kaouther', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Man Who Sold His Skin (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-01T11:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicolas Rapold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicolas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rapold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6d4a3ed-2f76-5251-bb95-d97be4243021', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6d4a3ed-2f76-5251-bb95-d97be4243021'}, {'abstract': 'This ethereal experimental documentary by Sky Hopinka is an essential portrait of contemporary Indigenous life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/01/movies/malni-towards-the-ocean-towards-the-shore-review.html', 'snippet': 'This ethereal experimental documentary by Sky Hopinka is an essential portrait of contemporary Indigenous life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An essential portrait of contemporary Indigenous life that resists the touristic gaze, “Małni — Towards the Ocean, Towards the Shore,” the debut feature from the Ho-Chunk artist and filmmaker Sky Hopinka, isn’t too concerned with whether we fully understand the traditions and rituals it entrancingly commits to screen. It refreshingly centers the Native perspective, and beckons audiences onto its wavelength by tapping into something more intuitive, the stuff of dreams.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/01/arts/01malni-art/01malni-art-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 314, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/01/arts/01malni-art/01malni-art-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 314}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/01/arts/01malni-art/01malni-art-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 536, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/01/arts/01malni-art/01malni-art-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1073, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/01/arts/01malni-art/01malni-art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/04/01/arts/01malni-art/01malni-art-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/01/arts/01malni-art/01malni-art-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Malni — Towards the Ocean, Towards the Shore’ Review: Embracing Our Ghosts', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Pick', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Malni — Towards the Ocean, Towards the Shore', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hopinka, Sky', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Malni -- Towards the Ocean, Towards the Shore (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pacific Northwestern States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Native Americans', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indigenous People', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mercier, Jordan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sahme, Sweetwater', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-01T11:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Beatrice Loayza', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beatrice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Loayza', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/18de0497-0b92-5634-9bb5-d535798f73af', 'word_count': 330, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18de0497-0b92-5634-9bb5-d535798f73af'}, {'abstract': 'Idris Elba leads us through the long-buried heritage of America’s Black cowboys, manifested in their modern-day urban descendants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/01/movies/concrete-cowboy-review-acquiring-horse-sense-on-the-philly-streets.html', 'snippet': 'Idris Elba leads us through the long-buried heritage of America’s Black cowboys, manifested in their modern-day urban descendants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a quote that’s been circulating for years and years, apocryphally attributed to Ronald Reagan, Winston Churchill and a few other white men: “There is nothing so good for the inside of a man than the outside of a horse.” In “Concrete Cowboy,” the improving aspects of horseback riding — and, yes, stable maintenance — are demonstrated in the tale of a troubled Black teenager, Cole (Caleb McLaughlin).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/02/arts/30CONCRETE-PIX/merlin_185547843_664b0622-cbb6-4bc9-b164-7c53fc2427b5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/02/arts/30CONCRETE-PIX/merlin_185547843_664b0622-cbb6-4bc9-b164-7c53fc2427b5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 404}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/02/arts/30CONCRETE-PIX/merlin_185547843_664b0622-cbb6-4bc9-b164-7c53fc2427b5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 690, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/02/arts/30CONCRETE-PIX/merlin_185547843_664b0622-cbb6-4bc9-b164-7c53fc2427b5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1381, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/02/arts/30CONCRETE-PIX/30CONCRETE-PIX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 't