ument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Cain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cf35137-5e36-57c0-80a3-a6d5a8cb9948', 'word_count': 1116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cf35137-5e36-57c0-80a3-a6d5a8cb9948'}, {'abstract': 'At this point in the pandemic, it feels that we have, all, collectively, hit a wall. Last week, The New York Times asked readers to tell us about work burnout they’re experiencing — nearly 700 people responded in two days. The responses were funny, vulnerable and indicative of a universal sense of: “We’ve had enough.” The collective picture they painted was of a work force struggling to do tasks that were once easy, people who know they are lucky to have a job but dream of quitting, and who would do anything to never have a Zoom meeting again.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/03/business/readers-pandemic-burnout.html', 'snippet': '', 'lead_paragraph': 'At this point in the pandemic, it feels that we have, all, collectively, hit a wall. Last week, The New York Times asked readers to tell us about work burnout they’re experiencing — nearly 700 people responded in two days. The responses were funny, vulnerable and indicative of a universal sense of: “We’ve had enough.” The collective picture they painted was of a work force struggling to do tasks that were once easy, people who know they are lucky to have a job but dream of quitting, and who would do anything to never have a Zoom meeting again.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Here’s What Readers Told Us About Feeling Burned Out', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-03T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophia June', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'June', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45789070-f1ae-5df9-a980-74e875ad4a25', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45789070-f1ae-5df9-a980-74e875ad4a25'}, {'abstract': 'You can’t stop your landlord from renting out common areas to a film crew, but you can try to get some concessions from the production.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/03/realestate/help-my-apartment-building-is-a-movie-set.html', 'snippet': 'You can’t stop your landlord from renting out common areas to a film crew, but you can try to get some concessions from the production.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q: Tenants at my New Paltz, N.Y., rental building were recently informed by our landlord that a production crew would be filming scenes for a major motion-picture on the property for four days later this month. No one asked us, yet we are being asked for our “understanding” and “cooperation,” which includes moving our vehicles from assigned parking spaces. During the busiest day, 70 people will be on site from 7 a.m. to 11 p.m., a violation of the local noise ordinance. Inconvenience aside, it sounds like a potential health hazard during the pandemic. Is this legal? What can be done?', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/04/realestate/03ASK-Illo/03ASK-Illo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 118, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/04/realestate/03ASK-Illo/03ASK-Illo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 118}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/04/realestate/03ASK-Illo/03ASK-Illo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 201, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/04/realestate/03ASK-Illo/03ASK-Illo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/04/realestate/03ASK-Illo/03ASK-Illo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/04/04/realestate/03ASK-Illo/03ASK-Illo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/04/realestate/03ASK-Illo/03ASK-Illo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Help! My Apartment Building Is a Movie Set', 'kicker': 'Ask Real Estate', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When a Building Becomes a Movie Set, What Can Tenants Do to Remain Safe?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Renting and Leasing (Real Estate)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Landlords', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Noise', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Content Type: Service', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-03T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ronda Kaysen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ronda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaysen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7304f84d-172a-55e3-b091-79e60f1b79c2', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7304f84d-172a-55e3-b091-79e60f1b79c2'}, {'abstract': 'The author of “The Paris Wife” made a name for herself exploring the lives of real women. In her first suspense novel, “When the Stars Go Dark,” she faces her own demons.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/03/books/paula-mclain-when-stars-go-dark.html', 'snippet': 'The author of “The Paris Wife” made a name for herself exploring the lives of real women. In her first suspense novel, “When the Stars Go Dark,” she faces her own demons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you talk to enough writers, you’ll notice a theme of books and words as saviors. You’ll hear how a meaningful novel landed, at just the right moment, in the hands of a person who was alone and scared; how a humble ballpoint pen became a microphone, a stage, a shield or a sword.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/05/books/13PAULAMCLAIN-04/merlin_185245557_71c61916-4b1b-41a2-8aac-28cbe89b8234-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/05/books/13PAULAMCLAIN-04/merlin_185245557_71c61916-4b1b-41a2-8aac-28cbe89b8234-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/