tudents for a life of religious scholarship — at the expense of basic reading, writing, math and science. New York State law mandates that private and religious schools provide a curriculum equivalent to that of public schools, and a 2019 report by New York City’s Department of Education found that only two of the 28 yeshivas it investigated met these requirements. This is especially problematic, considering that the city’s yeshivas receive over $100 million in state funds annually.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My Son’s Yeshiva Is Breaking the Law', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private and Sectarian Schools', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Orthodox Judaism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reading and Writing Skills (Education)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-07T09:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Beatrice Weber and Chaim Levin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Beatrice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Chaim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Levin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae8bd5ca-d93d-5018-ba90-b87691b4da73', 'word_count': 196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae8bd5ca-d93d-5018-ba90-b87691b4da73'}, {'abstract': 'When life is disrupted by crisis, some people see opportunities — for change, acti