': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Kingsley and Rana F. Sweis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kingsley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rana', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Sweis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/418f942b-5db3-5ce5-895f-02f65edf6199', 'word_count': 757, 'uri': 'nyt://article/418f942b-5db3-5ce5-895f-02f65edf6199'}, {'abstract': 'Luke Epplin talks about “Our Team,” which recounts the Cleveland Indians’ seasons of 1947 and 1948, including Larry Doby becoming the first Black player in the American League.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/07/books/our-team-luke-epplin-cleveland-indians-larry-doby-interview.html', 'snippet': 'Luke Epplin talks about “Our Team,” which recounts the Cleveland Indians’ seasons of 1947 and 1948, including Larry Doby becoming the first Black player in the American League.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whether or not you care about baseball, you know the name Jackie Robinson. But even baseball fans of a certain age might not know Larry Doby, who made his major-league debut with the Cleveland Indians on July 5, 1947, integrating the American League just three months after Robinson had courageously done the same for the National League. Doby’s experience as the second Black player to break baseball’s 20th-century color barrier is one strand of Luke Epplin’s first book, “Our Team.” Epplin recounts the Indians’ seasons of 1947 and 1948 through the stories of four iconic figures: Doby; Satchel Paige, the Negro leagues pitching legend who joined the Indians in 1948, at 42 years old; Bill Veeck, the team’s maverick owner; and Bob Feller, the Hall of Fame pitcher who had debuted in Cleveland in 1936, when he was just 17. Below, Epplin talks about the origins of his interest in the team, how Doby’s experience differed from Robinson’s and more.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/08/books/review/07BOOKEPPLIN1/07BOOKEPPLIN1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 862, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/08/books/review/07BOOKEPPLIN1/07BOOKEPPLIN1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 862}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/08/books/review/07BOOKEPPLIN1/07BOOKEPPLIN1-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 714, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/08/books/review/07BOOKEPPLIN1/07BOOKEPPLIN1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1427, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/08/books/review/07BOOKEPPLIN1/07BOOKEPPLIN1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/04/08/books/review/07BOOKEPPLIN1/07BOOKEPPLIN1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/08/books/review/07BOOKEPPLIN1/07BOOKEPPLIN1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Integration Came to the American League, in Cleveland’s Glory Days', 'kicker': '5 Things About Your Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How Integration Came to the American League', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doby, Larry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paige, Satchel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feller, Bob (1918-2010)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Veeck, Bill', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cleveland Indians', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Our Team: The Epic Story of Four Men and the World Series That Changed Baseball (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Segregation and Desegregation', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Epplin, Luke', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-07T17:23:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4d70d18-231c-5e91-8c90-2c721b238efd', 'word_count': 944, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4d70d18-231c-5e91-8c90-2c721b238efd'}, {'abstract': 'In New York opera circles, an autograph request from her, the mezzo-soprano Frederica von Stade said, was considered “a special type of approval.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/07/arts/music/lois-kirschenbaum-dead.html', 'snippet': 'In New York opera circles, an autograph request from her, the mezzo-soprano Frederica von Stade said, was considered “a special type of approval.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'For more than a half-century, nearly every prominent singer to per