pe': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sandra E. Garcia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sandra', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Garcia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d171b58a-7b7e-5f46-ac54-98c53667adff', 'word_count': 841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d171b58a-7b7e-5f46-ac54-98c53667adff'}, {'abstract': 'Jeremy O. Harris: From Visiting Student Day in December 2015 to March 2016, when we got our acceptance letters, I met a bunch of people who had heard that I was applying to the Yale School of Drama, because that’s how it starts: When Black playwrights hear that a Black playwright is applying, they reach out. I heard from fellow students and alums, including Phillip Howze, Jiréh Breon Holder, Tori Sampson and Josh Wilder. Once I got in, I felt emboldened, like I was already part of the program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/12/t-magazine/jeremy-o-harris-yale-drama.html', 'snippet': '', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeremy O. Harris: From Visiting Student Day in December 2015 to March 2016, when we got our acceptance letters, I met a bunch of people who had heard that I was applying to the Yale School of Drama, because that’s how it starts: When Black playwrights hear that a Black playwright is applying, they reach out. I heard from fellow students and alums, including Phillip Howze, Jiréh Breon Holder, Tori Sampson and Josh Wilder. Once I got in, I felt emboldened, like I was already part of the program.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '76', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/12/t-magazine/12tmag-harrisyaledrama/12tmag-harrisyaledrama-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/202