ool of Drama', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-12T09:02:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maya Phillips', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Phillips', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1848d10-79ab-5998-925a-ab222ffb160b', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1848d10-79ab-5998-925a-ab222ffb160b'}, {'abstract': 'For three years, the comedian Bowen Yang found community at Bubble_T, New York’s roving hub for queer Asian nightlife. Then the pandemic hit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/12/t-magazine/bowen-yang-bubble-t.html', 'snippet': 'For three years, the comedian Bowen Yang found community at Bubble_T, New York’s roving hub for queer Asian nightlife. Then the pandemic hit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bowen Yang: I met this group of friends mostly through social media and mutual friends, but I actually know Adrian from when we were both working at One Kings Lane five or six years ago. He and I kind of attuned ourselves to each other, the way that any Asian person, and specifically queer Asian person, will: “Oh, I see you.” And then the separate friendships converged at Bubble_T, which was a novel concept back in 2017, a roving party inspired by and catering to queer Asian folks but also inclusive and open to anybody.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '70', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/12/t-magazine/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/12/t-magazine/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/12/t-magazine/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/12/t-magazine/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1250, 'width': 1874, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/12/t-magazine/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/04/12/t-magazine/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/12/t-magazine/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy/12tmag-yangbubblet-copy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Party Friends', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Party Friends', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yang, Bowen (1990- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parties (Social)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asian-Americans', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality and Bisexuality', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-12T09:02:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5ffc132-44bc-5e44-aff8-32372ed4c90d', 'word_count': 454, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5ffc132-44bc-5e44-aff8-32372ed4c90d'}, {'abstract': 'In a year without access to queer spaces, one writer considers the importance of the bonds they helped form.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/12/t-magazine/friendship-queer-nightlife.html', 'snippet': 'In a year without access to queer spaces, one writer considers the importance of the bonds they helped form.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LATELY, I’VE BEEN checking in on the friends I’ve made in queer spaces. Without a physical nexus at which to gather, we’ve sort of scattered. It happens. But over the past decade, my time at the bars — and the coffee shops and the clubs and the warehouses and the bathhouses and the house parties and the dimly lit cafes spread across Texas, New Orleans, Japan and wherever else — was largely defined by the folks I met there, who became comrades for the evening, or for the rest of my life, whether we were at Ripcord or Blur or Rain or Grand Slam or Golden Ball or Charlies or Oz or Good Friends or Rawhide or Phoenix.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/12/t-magazine/12tmag-essays-slide-I18K/12tmag-essays-slide-I18K-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/12/t-magazine/12tmag-essays-slide-I18K/12tmag-essays-slide-I18K-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, '