: 'nyt://article/24d0981f-4717-554b-9e11-43f67e3965af', 'word_count': 1273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24d0981f-4717-554b-9e11-43f67e3965af'}, {'abstract': 'A Zoom lecture explores how ancient Greeks used alcohol to fuel their discussions, and how that culture informed their drinking vessels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/12/dining/greek-wine-drinking-lecture.html', 'snippet': 'A Zoom lecture explores how ancient Greeks used alcohol to fuel their discussions, and how that culture informed their drinking vessels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The symposium in ancient Greece was a formal postprandial drinking party for men, during which topics of interest were discussed as tongues were loosened. Plato has famously documented it. In its series, “Archaeology Abridged,” the Archaeological Institute of America is offering a Zoom lecture by Kathleen Lynch, a professor of classics at the University of Cincinnati, to explain the symposium and the influence of drinking on ancient pottery shapes.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/14/dining/14BurnerGreek/12BurnerGreek-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/14/dining/14BurnerGreek/12BurnerGreek-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/14/dining/14BurnerGreek/12BurnerGreek-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/14/dining/14BurnerGreek/12BurnerGreek-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/14/dining/14BurnerGreek/12BurnerGreek-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/04/14/dining/14BurnerGreek/12BurnerGreek-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/14/dining/14BurnerGreek/12BurnerGreek-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Symposium’s Saucy History', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Appreciate: The Greek Symposium Was Fueled With Alcohol', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greek Civilization', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Archaeology and Anthropology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tableware', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-12T22:22:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6268fd3-6578-5f44-8a5c-6e94f61275a7', 'word_count': 111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6268fd3-6578-5f44-8a5c-6e94f61275a7'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Simonson offers up scores of recipes for the Mexican spirit, and its cousin, tequila, in his latest book.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/12/dining/drinks/mezcal-and-tequila-cocktails-book.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Simonson offers up scores of recipes for the Mexican spirit, and its cousin, tequila, in his latest book.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You know who you’re dealing with when Robert Simonson, the author of “3-Ingredient Cocktails” and a New York Times contributor, suggests drinks made with mezcal and tequila. Simplicity reigns. Of the more than 60 cocktail recipes in his new book, “Mezcal and Tequila Cocktails,” most rely on mezcal, a good thing because though several tequila drinks are standard-issue, using mezcal is less common. Mr. Simonson starts with a timeline, remarking that today, mezcal’s standing in the world of spirits is what single-malts enjoyed in the 1990s. He describes how the agave spirits are made, then rolls out the recipes. Some, like the Mezcalexander and the Mezcal Sour are riffs on popular cocktails. Others, including the Elegante, combining mezcal and ginger liqueur, are lovely inventions. You do not have to concoct vats of arcane ingredients; what you need is in liquor stores and on grocery shelves.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/14/dining/14BurnerBook/merlin_186061935_215b9cf4-1402-495a-b34c-e038fff154ea-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 877, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/14/dining/14BurnerBook/merlin_186061935_215b9cf4-1402-495a-b34c-e038fff154ea-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 877}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/14/dining/14BurnerBook/merlin_186061935_215b9cf4-1402-495a-b34c-e038fff154ea-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 701, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/14/dining/14BurnerBook/merlin_186061935_215b9cf4-1402-495a-b34c-e038fff154ea-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1402, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'cro