Wednesday, April 14, 2021.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Because of an editing error, an article on Saturday about the causes of India’s most recent wave of coronavirus infections misstated India’s Covid-19 mortality rate. The country recorded 0.04 deaths per 100,000 people for the past seven days as of April 9. The figure did not represent a rate for total deaths since the pandemic began.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: April 14, 2021', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2021-04-14T02:17:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Corrections', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Correction', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce276c09-2045-5cfd-acf4-66f636ba9257', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce276c09-2045-5cfd-acf4-66f636ba9257'}, {'abstract': 'Cariol Horne acted to keep a white officer from using what she saw as excessive force. Fifteen years later, a judge said her firing was wrong.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/13/nyregion/cariol-horne-police-chokehold.html', 'snippet': 'Cariol Horne acted to keep a white officer from using what she saw as excessive force. Fifteen years later, a judge said her firing was wrong.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a cold November day in Buffalo when Officer Cariol Horne responded to a call for a colleague in need of help. What she encountered was a white officer who appeared to be “in a rage” punching a handcuffed Black man in the face repeatedly as other officers stood by.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/13/nyregion/13buffalo/13buffalo-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width'