None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa38034a-14e9-5f39-8cda-be6eaff581f2', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa38034a-14e9-5f39-8cda-be6eaff581f2'}, {'abstract': 'Videos of a mysterious celestial phenomenon captured a once-common human emotion: awe at the wonder of the heavens.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/15/magazine/space-x-debris.html', 'snippet': 'Videos of a mysterious celestial phenomenon captured a once-common human emotion: awe at the wonder of the heavens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At around 9 o’clock on the night of March 25, the sky above my house in Seattle lit up with an astonishing display. I know this not because I happened to see it — I was inside, as I am too often, working at my computer — but because my Twitter feed was suddenly full of different versions of the same, uncanny video. The earliest clips captured what looked like a single streak of light, fracturing outward into a shimmering cascade. What came next looked like the world’s largest and longest-lasting firework, or a huge shower of comets hitting all at once, or a waterfall of light falling sideways. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/18/magazine/18mag-screenland/18mag-screenland-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/18/magazine/18mag-screenland/18mag-screenland-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/18/magazine/18mag-screenland/18mag-screenland-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'sup