eb', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca5c3313-4590-5d97-94b9-137d4ad933eb'}, {'abstract': 'The men and women who went to work and war during World War II were backed by a care economy. We need one too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/16/opinion/care-economy-infrastructure-rosie-the-riveter.html', 'snippet': 'The men and women who went to work and war during World War II were backed by a care economy. We need one too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I have spent the past two weeks reading about the infrastructure debate with mounting rage. When a reporter asked me recently why I felt so strongly, I sputtered, groping for the words. Ultimately, however, the answer is simple. Insisting that there is actually a fixed definition of what infrastructure is — bridges, but not baby care — perfectly encapsulates the ways in which the world is still shaped by men. Not just conservative men, but men across the political spectrum. Men today, that is: Three-quarters of a century ago, the Greatest Generation recognized that both forms of infrastructure were essential to the war effort.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/20/opinion/20slaughter-inyt/16Slaughter-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/20/opinion/20slaughter-inyt/16Slaughter-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/20/opinion/20slaughter-inyt/16Slaughter-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/20/opinion/20slaughter-inyt/16Slaughter-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1600, 'width': 1600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/20/opinion/20slaughter-inyt/16Slaughter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/04/20/opinion/20slaughter-inyt/16Slaughter-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/20/opinion/20slaughter-inyt/16Slaughter-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rosie Could Be a Riveter Only Because of a Care Economy. Where Is Ours?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Child Care Is Every Bit as Essential as Bridges', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biden, Joseph R Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'American Jobs Plan (2021)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Care', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-16T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne-Marie Slaughter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne-marie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Slaughter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5c4e7c3-2b91-56c6-8be1-ee3967f2c86d', 'word_count': 1189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5c4e7c3-2b91-56c6-8be1-ee3967f2c86d'}, {'abstract': 'When Carla Kitzmiller and John Porter’s April 1 wedding seemed impossible to pull off, Ms. Kitzmiller’s co-workers helped to make it happen at the car dealership where she works.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/16/style/carla-kitzmiller-john-porter-wedding.html', 'snippet': 'When Carla Kitzmiller and John Porter’s April 1 wedding seemed impossible to pull off, Ms. Kitzmiller’s co-workers helped to make it happen at the car dealership where she works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In early March, Carla Kitzmiller, a title clerk at the Indiana car dealership Thomas Kia of Highland, went to work with a problem. She and her fiancé, John Porter, wanted to be married April 1, but because of Covid regulations, their options seemed limited to eloping at a courthouse near their home in Munster, Ind. “The girls I work with started talking, and they were like, ‘No! You can’t have family at the courthouse,’” Ms. Kitzmiller said.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/18/fashion/00KitzmillerPorter/00KitzmillerPorter-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/18/fashion/00KitzmillerPorter/00KitzmillerPorter-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/18/fashion/00KitzmillerPorter/00KitzmillerPorter-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop