Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles, Giovanni Russonello and Lindsay Zoladz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Giovanni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Russonello', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Lindsay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zoladz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c921e3d4-230d-58b2-9e85-dbdc547ea9ed', 'word_count': 1318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c921e3d4-230d-58b2-9e85-dbdc547ea9ed'}, {'abstract': 'Vir Das, Brian Regan, Erica Rhodes and Ester Steinberg each find new ways to make a virtue out of the necessity of performing al fresco in a pandemic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/16/arts/television/outdoor-comedy-specials.html', 'snippet': 'Vir Das, Brian Regan, Erica Rhodes and Ester Steinberg each find new ways to make a virtue out of the necessity of performing al fresco in a pandemic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Laughter doesn’t echo off clouds. That’s the first challenge of outdoor comedy. According to common wisdom, the ideal conditions for stand-up — small dark room, low ceiling — are pretty much the opposite of al fresco comedy. There was actually a history of such performing, before the pandemic, with its own street-comedy legends. But in the past year, a niche became mainstream, and now, there’s a new genre of special, tried by Chelsea Handler, Colin Quinn and others. Four more funny comedians have recently gotten laughs taking the special outside, and considering the loosening of rules for indoor performance, they could also be the last of their kind.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia