(Afghanistan)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-18T15:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas L. Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b43bcb3f-bbaa-50da-9934-2c4025a9d9d1', 'word_count': 2182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b43bcb3f-bbaa-50da-9934-2c4025a9d9d1'}, {'abstract': 'Social media companies and other organizations are looking out for themselves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/18/opinion/cancel-culture-social-media.html', 'snippet': 'Social media companies and other organizations are looking out for themselves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In March, Alexi McCammond, the newly hired editor of Teen Vogue, resigned following backlash over offensive tweets she’d sent a decade ago, beginning when she was 17. In January, Will Wilkinson lost his job as vice president for research at the center-right Niskanen Center for a satirical tweet about Republicans who wanted to hang Mike Pence. (Wilkinson was also suspended from his role as a Times Opinion contributor.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/18/opinion/18klein-lead/18klein-lead-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 539, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/18/opinion/18klein-lead/18klein-lead-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 539}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/18/opinion/18klein-lead/18klein-lead-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 920, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name'