'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/19/us/politics/19new-washington-sectarianism1/19sectarianism1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Analysis: Sectarianism is a growing threat to U.S. democracy, as camps increasingly see the other as an enemy.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2021-04-19T14:50:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Cohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be68a75d-75fd-5944-b8d9-2775406e2464', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be68a75d-75fd-5944-b8d9-2775406e2464'}, {'abstract': 'The pastry chef Ursula Sala-Illa, a.k.a. Ursula XVII, sells bonbons, bars and cookies at Atelier Disset in Cutchogue, N.Y.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/19/dining/disset-chocolate-north-fork.html', 'snippet': 'The pastry chef Ursula Sala-Illa, a.k.a. Ursula XVII, sells bonbons, bars and cookies at Atelier Disset in Cutchogue, N.Y.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ursula Sala-Illa, who prefers to be known as Ursula XVII, has just opened her own chocolate shop, Atelier Disset, on the North Fork of Long Island. The pastry chef’s complicated nomenclature has to do with her Catalonian roots. In the Catalan language “disset” means 17 (pronounce it and if you know French, you’ll also understand). Ursula XVII — a native New Yorker who worked at Ai Fiori in New York and with Albert Adrià and Oriol Balaguer in Spain — has settled on the North Fork. On its website and in-store, the compact little shop sells finely crafted and decorated bonbon assortments with fillings like coconut marzipan and banana walnut, assorted bars and chunky cookies. Eventually she plans to hold workshops.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/21/dining/19Burner-chocolate/merlin_186480057_453a2177-f1aa-446e-99d7-d03dc9cf9c4f-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/21/dining/19Burner-chocolate/merlin_186480057_453a2177-f1aa-446e-99d7-d03dc9cf9c4f-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/21/dining/19Burner-chocolate/merlin_186480057_453a2177-f1aa-446e-99d7-d03dc9cf9c4f-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 660, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/21/dining/19Burner-chocolate/merlin_186480057_453a2177-f1aa-446e-99d7-d03dc9cf9c4f-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1321, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/21/dining/19Burner-chocolate/19Burner-chocolate-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/04/21/dining/19Burner-chocolate/19Burner-chocolate-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/21/dining/19Burner-chocolate/19Burner-chocolate-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Whimsical Chocolate Shop for the North Fork', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Nibble: The North Fork Gets A Whimsical Shop', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chocolate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cutchogue (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-19T14:50:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cafcec20-ab29-5894-a6ad-197163b0ace3', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cafcec20-ab29-5894-a6ad-197163b0ace3'}, {'abstract': 'The cookbook author Diane Kochilas now sells grape leaves from Crete for dolmas and fish.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/19/dining/easter-dolmas-grape-leaves.html', 'snippet': 'The cookbook author Diane Kochilas now sells grape leaves from Crete for dolmas and fish.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This year, Eastern Orthodox Easter comes on May 2. In a run-up to the celebration, there are some ingredients for traditional dishes that are worth having on hand, notably grape leaves, used for enclosing fillings to make dolmas (vine leaves stuffed with grains, meat or seafood) or to wrap fish before baking. The Greek food expert Diane Kochilas has recently added organic grape leaves from Crete to her website, along with recipes.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/21/dining/19burner-leaf/19burner-leaf-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/21/dining/19burner-leaf/19burner-leaf-articleLarg