lue': 'Cleave: Poems (Book)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Oh You Robot Saints! (Book)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Skin: Poems (Book)', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-20T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4704e9e-29d1-556d-a00f-8631dfa5a80f', 'word_count': 414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4704e9e-29d1-556d-a00f-8631dfa5a80f'}, {'abstract': 'As much as cultures have changed over the years, we still share traits with the flawed, searching characters between these covers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/20/books/review/cathedral-ben-hopkins.html', 'snippet': 'As much as cultures have changed over the years, we still share traits with the flawed, searching characters between these covers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With its intersecting plots that rest on the slow but not so steady construction of an enormous church in medieval Alsace, Ben Hopkins’s novel has the look of an old-fashioned doorstop of a saga. But when you immerse yourself in the shifting perspectives of CATHEDRAL (Europa, 624 pp., $28), what you also discover is a clever (even postmodern?) commentary on the ironies of history. As he traces the fates of a lively scrum of characters, Hopkins shows how the seemingly firm hierarchies of one era can be grounded in the social strife — and sometimes sheer luck — of those that came before. In this nimble mesh of stories, a trading empire can have its roots in something as simple as the theft of a wig. And forbidden love can lead to a lonely death or to the birth, many years onward, of a whole new community.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/02/books/review/02Becker/02Becker-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 212, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/02/books/review/02Becker/02Becker-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 212}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/02/books/review/02Becker/02Becker-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/02/books/review/02Becker/02Becker-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/02/books/review/02Becker/02Becker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/05/02/books/review/02Becker/02Becker-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/02/books/review/02Becker/02Becker-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Spanning Centuries and Continents, Novels of Our Common Humanity', 'kicker': 'Fiction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ex Cathedra', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Cathedral (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Children's Train (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hopkins, Ben (Author)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Adventures and Misadventures of the Extraordinary and Admirable Joan Orpi, Conquistador and Founder of New Catalonia (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Besora, Max', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ardone, Viola', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-20T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alida Becker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alida', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Becker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8973363-3a05-5d82-ad3f-3b8c7b3273d4', 'word_count': 981, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8973363-3a05-5d82-ad3f-3b8c7b3273d4'}, {'abstract': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on dealing with a parent radicalized by a recommendation engine and whether to cut off a friend who has joined a cult.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/20/magazine/youtube-radicalization.html', 'snippet': 'The magazine’s Ethicist columnist on dealing with a parent radicalized by a recommendation engine and whether to cut off a friend who has joined a cult.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For nearly his entire life, my 80-something-year-old father has been a quiet, gentle and deeply religious man who went to Mass and said the rosary daily. Although his political views have always been conservative, he has also always believed in kindness and fairness. Since the start of the pandemic, his social interactions have become severely reduced, limited to daily calls from me (I live across the country), weekly visits from my brother and the occasional shopping trips and church attendance. As our mother passed away before the pandemic, his one loyal companion has been his iPad and YouTube. Because of his viewing of religious programs, YouTube has increasingly steered him toward conservative media, so that he is now obsessed with right-wing extremist politics and is absolutely against taking the Covid vaccine. Every time my brother or I have a conversation with him, he talks politics and pushes his views, and even after we asked him to stop, he tries to get the last word in by sending us angry emails or texts. Now both of us try to avoid having any interactions with him. I have the password to his YouTube account from a year ago when I helped him with a tech problem. In order to preserve our relationship, I’m thinking about going into his account to delete and pause his viewing history, and perhaps put in some links to more wholesome entertainment such as music and soccer to counter the constant bombardment of extremism. My justification is that if he is being brainwashed by an algorithm, then I might as well use the algorithm to steer him back to his old self so that we can at least have a normal conversation. What is your view on this? Name Withheld', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/25/magazine/25mag-ethicist/25mag-ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/25/magazine/25mag-ethicist/25mag-ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/25/magazine/25mag-ethicist/25mag-ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/25/magazine/25mag-ethicist/25mag-ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1148, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/25/magazine/25mag-ethicist/25mag-ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/04/25/magazine/25mag-ethicist/25mag-ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/25/magazine/25mag-ethicist/25mag-ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'YouTube Videos Brainwashed My Father. Can I Reprogram His Feed?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Father Has Been Brainwashed by YouTube Videos. Can I Reprogram His Feed?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religious Cults', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-20T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kwame Anthony Appiah', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kwame', 'middlename': 'Anthony', 'lastname': 'Appiah', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/62a08c28-47f0-518c-be53-495057ca708b', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62a08c28-47f0-518c-be53-495057ca708b'}, {'abstract': 'To help explain to children the urgency of a warmer planet, a visual journalist created artwork with a playful approach.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/20/insider/climate-change-children.html', 'snippet': 'To help explain to children the urgency of a warmer p