irstname': 'Emma', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Fitzsimmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb7a0eca-f67e-5235-8204-5b328f9bce30', 'word_count': 806, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb7a0eca-f67e-5235-8204-5b328f9bce30'}, {'abstract': 'Dystopian streaming shows like “Made for Love” and “The One” imagine what happens when Big Tech creeps into finding soul mates and fixing marriages.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/21/arts/television/dystopian-tech-soulmates.html', 'snippet': 'Dystopian streaming shows like “Made for Love” and “The One” imagine what happens when Big Tech creeps into finding soul mates and fixing marriages.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Delete Hinge, Bumble, Grindr, Raya. Tell OkCupid to put its digital arrows away. To find true love, pluck a hair, seal it in a baggie, place the baggie inside a dedicated mailer and wait for your phone to chime with an update on your perfect match. This is the biotech fantasy of “The One,” a Netflix thriller. On “Soulmates,” last year’s anthology series on AMC, your data is uploaded via a painless retinal scan, but the life partner payoff remains the same.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/22/arts/00techlove-madeforlove/merlin_185768157_befe546c-b8c4-482f-9764-3cd00637ae68-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/22/arts/00techlove-madeforlove/merlin_185768157_befe546c-b8c4-482f-9764-3cd00637ae68-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/22/arts/00techlove-madeforlove/merlin_185768157_befe546c-b8c4-482f-9764-3cd00637ae6