lture)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-23T09:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivian Marino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/811c8f0b-9487-5481-bb17-6e566bbc7cfd', 'word_count': 1401, 'uri': 'nyt://article/811c8f0b-9487-5481-bb17-6e566bbc7cfd'}, {'abstract': 'From Falcon to Black Panther to a potential new Superman, a wave of rejuvenated heroes, reimagined by Black creators, are rewriting superhero mythologies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/23/arts/black-superheroes.html', 'snippet': 'From Falcon to Black Panther to a potential new Superman, a wave of rejuvenated heroes, reimagined by Black creators, are rewriting superhero mythologies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After the success of the Star Wars series “The Mandalorian” and the Marvel series “WandaVision,” the bar was set high for “The Falcon and the Winter Soldier.” But the series that focuses on two secondary characters — one Black and one white — from the Captain America comic book story line surpassed those expectations as the most watched premiere in Disney+ history. As the show’s first season comes to an end on Friday, its success can be seen as part of a wave of Black superheroes that have conquered our screens and comic book pages in recent years, in numbers — and with a nuance — never seen before.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Blackness Is a Superpower', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Black Heroes Reimagine The Fight For Equality', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marvel Comics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Disney Plus', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Falcon and the Winter Soldier (TV Program)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-23T09:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Veronica Chambers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Veronica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chambers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c269731-c498-5950-89b5-ddd05b3eaa1d', 'word_count': 1949, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c269731-c498-5950-89b5-ddd05b3eaa1d'}, {'abstract': 'As renewed rumors of a Black Superman movie swirl, here is the complicated history of reimagining the most iconic comic hero as a Black man.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/23/arts/black-superman.html', 'snippet': 'As renewed rumors of a Black Superman movie swirl, here is the complicated history of reimagining the most iconic comic hero as a Black man.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2008, during his presidential campaign, Barack Obama told a joke at a charity event. “Contrary to the rumors you have heard, I was not born in a manger,” he said. “I was actually born on Krypton and sent here by my father, Jor-El, to save the planet Earth.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/23/multimedia/23blackhistory-gustines1/merlin_186389334_8b6f0e5f-5e6f-413a-b27d-10ba14e57a52-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 913, 'width': 