ow did a Promised Land to generations of Black families become a community of lost lives?', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a wrinkled sheet of lined paper tucked in my mom’s purse was a list of places we had jotted down, memories to visit. It was January 2020, and my mother and I were on a trip to Chicago, to see the places from her past and my early childhood in and around the Englewood section of the city, where she was born and raised, and where I was, too, before we moved to Denver in 1969. As we drove south in our rental car, from Roosevelt University on South Michigan Avenue, where Mom attended college in the 1950s, toward what was called the Black Belt, I imagined myself as a 7-year-old sitting on the second-floor balcony of my great-aunt’s brick building on South Vernon Avenue, watching people bustle below. I would strain to hear my mother soaking in news and gossip from her aunties, their voices soft and Southern, before I was shooed away, warned to stay out of grown folks’ business.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/02/magazine/02RacialGap-image1/02RacialGap-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/05/02/magazine/02RacialGap-image1/02RacialGap-image1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 730}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/02/magazine/02RacialGap-image1/02RacialGap-image1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 842, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/02/magazine/02RacialGap-image1/02RacialGap-image1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1683, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/02/magazine/02RacialGap-image1/02RacialGap-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/05/02/magazine/02RacialGap-image1/02RacialGap-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/05/02/magazine/02RacialGap-image1/02RacialGap-image1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Black Lives Are Shorter in Chicago. My Family’s History Shows Why.', 'kicker': 'The Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life and Death in Englewood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Segregation and Desegregation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-27T09:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Villarosa', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Villarosa', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/941344c5-466b-5808-a