American Right Now?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'internal-essential', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asian-Americans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hate Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-28T14:39:32+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/17cea57a-a98d-5ba9-8fca-d165342fa59b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/17cea57a-a98d-5ba9-8fca-d165342fa59b'}, {'abstract': 'New research is intensifying the debate — with profound implications for the future of the planet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/28/magazine/human-lifespan.html', 'snippet': 'New research is intensifying the debate — with profound implications for the future of the planet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1990, not long after Jean-Marie Robine and Michel Allard began conducting a nationwide study of French centenarians, one of their software programs spat out an error message. An individual in the study was marked as 115 years old, a number outside the program’s range of acceptable age values. They called their collaborators in Arles, where the subject lived, and asked them to double-check the information they had provided, recalls Allard, who was then the director of the IPSEN Foundation, a nonprofit research organization. Perhaps they made a mistake when transcribing her birth date? Maybe this Jeanne Calment was actually born in 1885, not 1875? No, the collaborators said. We’ve seen her birth certificate. The data is correct.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Long Can We Live?', 'kicker': 'The Health Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Age-Old Question', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Longevity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stem Cells', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Calment, Jeanne', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sinclair, David Andrew (1969- )', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Olshansky, S Jay', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-28T14:51:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ferris Jabr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ferris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jabr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e6fdaeb-f67f-5c17-b0ae-21b0c09990f8', 'word_count': 4659, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e6fdaeb-f67f-5c17-b0ae-21b0c09990f8'}, {'abstract': 'The new bill, which some fear will curtail civil liberties, comes after a series of attacks and as the far right is stoking feelings of insecurity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/28/world/europe/france-antiterrorism-bill.html', 'snippet': 'The new bill, which some fear will curtail civil liberties, comes after a series of attacks and as the far right is stoking feelings of insecurity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — The French government, responding to several attack