section_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/118344f0-12ec-51d7-ad03-dcdcd4f9814f', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/118344f0-12ec-51d7-ad03-dcdcd4f9814f'}, {'abstract': 'Dawoud Bey’s images at the Whitney Museum expose rich histories hidden beneath the surface, how places evolve over time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/29/arts/design/dawoud-bey-whitney-museum-review.html', 'snippet': 'Dawoud Bey’s images at the Whitney Museum expose rich histories hidden beneath the surface, how places evolve over time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before he became a photographer, Dawoud Bey trained as a jazz percussionist, looking to John Coltrane as a role model for melding craft with a commitment to social justice. As a teenager in the 1960s, Bey was finely attuned to the social and political upheavals of the civil rights movement, staging sit-ins and demonstrations with his high school classmates and joining the Black Panther Party, whose newspaper he sold on the weekends. By 1968, the struggle for racial equality was converging with demonstrations against the war in Vietnam and the early stages of women’s liberation, forming a pattern of transformation and upheaval that culminated with the assassination of the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/30/arts/30dawoud-bey-1/merlin_186723027_61988f3f-1e7a-4797-bffe-f398c622b51d-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 889, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/30/arts/30dawoud-bey-1/merlin_186723027_61988f3f-1e7a-4797-bffe-f398c622b51d-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 889}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/30/arts/30dawoud-bey-1/merlin_186723027_61988f3f-1e7a-4797-bffe-f398c622b51d-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 691, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/30/arts/30dawoud-bey-1/merlin_186723027_61988f3f-1e7a-4797-bffe-f398c622b51d-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1382, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/30/arts/30dawoud-bey-1/00dawoud-bey-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/04/30/arts/30dawoud-bey-1/00dawoud-bey-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/30/arts/30dawoud-bey-1/00dawoud-bey-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Photographer Looks Deep Into America’s Past', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Pick', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beneath A Surface, History Can Hide', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bey, Dawoud', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Whitney Museum of American Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Harlem (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'n