l', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/189399b1-19cb-501f-ac7a-6a69bc71e727', 'word_count': 972, 'uri': 'nyt://article/189399b1-19cb-501f-ac7a-6a69bc71e727'}, {'abstract': 'Once again, tiny numbers of Black and Latino students received offers to attend New York City’s elite public high schools.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/29/nyregion/stuyvestant-black-students.html', 'snippet': 'Once again, tiny numbers of Black and Latino students received offers to attend New York City’s elite public high schools.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After a year in which the pandemic shined a harsh spotlight on the stark inequities in New York City’s school system, the city announced Thursday that, once again, only tiny numbers of Black and Latino students had been admitted into top public high schools. The numbers represent the latest signal that efforts to desegregate those schools while maintaining an admissions exam are failing.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/29/us/29nyspecialized-1/29nyspecialized-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/04/29/us/29nyspecialized-1/29nyspecialized-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 404}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/29/us/29nyspecialized-1/29nyspecialized-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 690, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/04/29/us/29nyspecialized-