 and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-04-30T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/fefa1b64-4c6c-5ab5-b761-9a6391a76ea6', 'word_count': 729, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fefa1b64-4c6c-5ab5-b761-9a6391a76ea6'}, {'abstract': 'Life expectancy doubled over the last century. Radically extending life is possible in this one. How long would you want to live?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/04/30/learning/would-you-want-to-live-to-200.html', 'snippet': 'Life expectancy doubled over the last century. Radically extending life is possible in this one. How long would you want to live?', 'lead_paragraph': 'From 1920 to 2020, the average human life span doubled. Medical and social advances such as the development of vaccines, germ theory and antibiotics, chlorinated water and pasteurized milk have reduced childhood deaths, mitigated diseases of old age and vastly prolonged life. The United Nations estimates that there were about 95,000 centenarians (people who live to 100) in 1990 and more than 450,000 in 2015.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Would You Want to Live to 200?', 'kicker': 'student opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2021-04-30T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy Engle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Engle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96463851-690f-50f9-91d9-36a050b3586b', 'word_count': 1126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96463851-690f-50f9-9